@flexiarg argument-coherence/plan-at-reviewable-grain
@title Plan at a Grain the Reviewer Can Check
@keywords plan, activities, breakdown, schedule, monitoring
@audience paper and proposal authors, reviewers, who-benefits analysts
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Mined from the Peeragogy "Greatest Flops" archive (~/notes/peeragogy-flops): the Wikimedia IEG 2013 comments on flop 5 (appendices/05, 'Comments'). Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; to test blind on the other flops, with Corneli 2016 (ICCC) and the Peeragogy Handbook as controls that should pass
@references [argument-coherence/ARGUMENT argument-coherence/evidence-the-venue-counts argument-coherence/earn-the-coined-term argument-coherence/one-worked-instance argument-coherence/say-how-it-carries-back argument-coherence/show-the-deliverable argument-coherence/claims-as-hypotheses]

! conclusion:
  A proposal breaks its activities down to a grain at which the reviewer can see who does what, in what order, and how progress would be visible.

  + context:
    Plans written by a team that knows its own habits read as complete to the team and vague to outsiders.

  + IF:
    The argument asks for support for a programme of work.

  + HOWEVER:
    Too fine a plan is brittle and invites micromanagement.

  + THEN:
    List the activities as distinct units, each with an owner and an observable output, and keep the list short.

  + BECAUSE:
    Flop 5's reviewer: 'Can you break down your activities a bit more clearly in the activities section?'

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Test blind: a reviewer given only a flop and this family; compare its rows with the recorded verdicts.]
